# wren's entry, written as data (https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1536):
# the compiler the framework's generic entry runs, its rounds and its
# backend. The JIT's options (dispatch fallback, the closure backend, a
# predicate per code, the instance table on, a compilation budget of 10000,
# the profitable checkpoints, no native blocks, applications by layer, the
# continuations read through their closed pack (#1794), the weak-head tables
# in the reading's state) and each round's implementations
# (the standard indexer, the higher-order saturator, the narrow block
# extractor with the measured bounds of gezi.strategies.JointReading.measured,
# the baseline lemma encoding, the closures' e-matching) are
# written out: there is no default. The executor that reads the programs is
# the framework's JIT (the executor jit of
# corpora/compiler/entry_options/executor).
#
# The library: the Prolog backend with the native typing it supports
# (prolog_backend_preset), closure_application and occurrence_erasure, the
# binary naturals' backend with wren's typing of them (wren_binary_preset:
# their codecs and natives, the typed versions' bindings to natives and
# unfoldings), and the files of the three rounds, wren's typed branch, its
# specialization and the lift of a function's own halving loop to iterate,
# each a member of wren_binary_preset. Native typing is enabled only through
# the two presets (rule 43 of docs/design/gezi-contest-hard.md).
#
# Four lemma rounds on one e-graph, each run to its fixed point: the typed
# branch (wren_typed_block); its specialization (wren_typed_specialization),
# which reduces the typed lets; the lift of each own halving loop to iterate
# applied to its function (wren_own_loop_lifting), which needs the lets
# reduced; and the specialization again, whose static argument lemma types
# the lifted loops. The unfoldings are left out, since the narrow blocks
# call the typed versions as natives. Each round's termination is its
# module's argument, written in the module's header (derived, not proved).
# Measured 2026-11-07 (the judge's practice runs, wren's draws from seed
# 20261031, CPUs 2 and 3, every point success, no round capped): the
# inlined hot shape compiles, 10k 0.40 and 0.79 s, 100k 1.36 and 0.46 s, 1M
# 0.63 and 0.99 s, against about 14 s and the budget with two rounds; on
# round 21's hot ladder, saturation over the eight points 1.37 and 1.30 s
# against 1.15 and 1.06 s with two rounds, wall sums 3.40 and 3.43 s against
# 3.09 and 3.46 s.
# Both specialization rounds open closures (#1270), so that the static
# argument lemma reaches the nested shape's inner loop, λt. iterate k G t,
# written η-expanded with iterate let-bound. Measured 2026-11-07 with #1270
# (CPUs 2 and 3): on round 25's points the six nested points compile to one
# block each, read in 207 to 403 steps, rounds 3, 102, 24, 0 instances,
# none capped; on round 23's points, the judge's practice run answers the
# six nested points in 0.14 to 0.95 s (stopped at 60 s without opening, 10k
# direct 21.57 s) and the whole set 111 success against 108.
# Every round enables every built-in rule (`rules`), as every round did
# before the rules were opt-in. Every round's leaves are those of every
# knowledge file of the entry (a contribution to the slot leaves that reads every file's leaves), as they were when the
# leaves were the library's and not a round's; a round's lemmas are its own
# file's. The backend is the choice of the same files' knowledge, of which
# the backend's slots are read.
# The file is read by projection off the tree whose visible subtree is
# Gezi's directory (StandardLibrary.entryTerm), in the scope of
# gezi; it imports the knowledge files at its top, each file's
# tuple's fields bound by position
# (https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1509, section 10.2).
